INSERT OR IGNORE INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, body, cover_image, category, author_name, published_at, status) VALUES
  ('the-best-digital-ecosystem-for-restaurants', 'The Best Digital Ecosystem for Restaurants', 'Consumers shape the modern restaurant experience. Technology advancements push marketplace trends toward a touch-free dining experience that seamlessly connects the front- and back-of-house. As more restaurants invest...', '[{"type":"p","text":"Consumers shape the modern restaurant experience. Technology advancements push marketplace trends toward a touch-free dining experience that seamlessly connects the front- and back-of-house. As more restaurants invest in cutting-edge technology like the Point of Sale system powered by eatOS, customers begin to expect the convenience that a good digital ecosystem provides."},{"type":"p","text":"Austin Distel on Unsplash"},{"type":"p","text":"With an advanced technological ecosystem, you can manage all your channels at once from one intuitive, interactive screen. From your Point of Sale to your tablets to all the other devices that make your operations run smoothly, give your customers the positive experience and brand consistency they expect."},{"type":"h2","text":"Built-In Flexibility"},{"type":"p","text":"The only sure thing in the restaurant marketplace is that trends will change. Customizable POS lets you prioritize the customer-facing screens and servers’ POS however you need. As times change, digital menus make it easy to adapt to seasonal ingredients or update prices alongside the competition. At eatOS, our system is also equipped to grow with the business so you can scale or open new locations and get side-by-side location reporting."},{"type":"p","text":"Automatic upselling capabilities prompt modifiers depending on what customers choose, so servers never miss a sales opportunity. Order notes make it easy to input any additional information, like allergies, so patrons get exactly what they want every time."},{"type":"p","text":"The future of foodservice will expand on everything from self-service to AI technology. Make sure your restaurant is equipped to grow with the times, wherever the industry goes next."},{"type":"h2","text":"Digital Point of Sale"},{"type":"p","text":"Point of Sales that work over the internet create a seamless connection between the front and back of house. The ideal POS connects with every device on the network simultaneously so all your employees can optimize whatever technology they rely on for work. This has the dual purpose of bettering their performance and collecting all your data for real-time generated reports. Keep track of sales trends to create smarter budgets, streamline operations wherever possible and even reduce labor costs with integrated scheduling software. When you know when the rush comes or the traffic is slow, you won’t have to worry about over- or understaffing ever again."},{"type":"h2","text":"Self Service"},{"type":"p","text":"Giving customers the chance to complete their own order from start to finish is a natural part of advancing forward. With tools to decrease labor expenses and hasten turnaround, technology integration pays off."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Kiosks work in-store to divert customers away from the cashier’s line and over to these devices instead. When customers review their tickets before submission, order accuracy increases."},{"type":"p","text":"2. Mobile ordering works at the table from home so customers have a completely contact-free experience."},{"type":"p","text":"3. Touch-free payments work thanks to agnostic processors that take most credit cards, mobile wallets, and NFC transactions."},{"type":"p","text":"Contactless self-service gives servers more time to spend out on the floor, talking directly with customers. The more they learn about your loyal guests, you can develop promotions and marketing campaigns you know will attract sales. Keep it all straight with customer profiles built on the smartest POS on the market."},{"type":"p","text":"Self-service technology is growing more and more popular, particularly as the COVID-19 pandemic makes social distancing important. Customers want a convenient, smart, touch-free dining experience achieved with a technological ecosystem powered by eatOS."},{"type":"p","text":"eatOS offers an all-in-one cloud-based eco-system exclusively for restaurants. From Point of Sale to Contactless Ordering, Pay & Order at Table, Kitchen Display System, Guest Facing Display, Online Ordering APP / Website and Workforce Management there is everything to help a restaurant succeed no matter the size. Click Here to Schedule a demo to learn more about how your restaurant can make a smooth transition into the world of restaurant technology made simple."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #Restaurant #RestaurantBusiness"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_0aebf752ef1b4882bdcb230bfad6cc07~mv2.jpeg', 'Point of Sale', 'eatOS Staff', '2022-04-05', 'published'),
  ('the-complete-guide-to-setting-up-restaurant-payroll', 'The Complete Guide to Setting Up Restaurant Payroll', 'If you’re new to launching a restaurant, necessary business operations can seem completely foreign and, as a result, insurmountably hard to understandlet alone trying to actually do it. As far as the federal, state and...', '[{"type":"p","text":"If you’re new to launching a restaurant, necessary business operations can seem completely foreign and, as a result, insurmountably hard to understandlet alone trying to actually do it. As far as the federal, state and local governments, as well as your employees, are concerned, though, managing your payroll system is one of the most important jobs you have."},{"type":"p","text":"What do you need to get payroll started at your new restaurant?"},{"type":"h2","text":"1\\. EIN"},{"type":"p","text":"Getting an EIN, or Employer Identification Number, is the first step towards identifying yourself as a legitimate business in the eyes of the federal government. This number, also known as your Employer Tax ID, establishes you as a business entity which allows you to open a company bank account, apply for licenses and file your tax returns. You can get an EIN online through the IRS."},{"type":"p","text":"Along the same lines, it’s important to note that some state and local governments require a business ID for their tax purposes too. You should contact your officials to see if you need one and learn how to apply."},{"type":"h2","text":"2\\. Staff Information"},{"type":"p","text":"To properly file your necessary payroll paperwork, youll need personal information from your staff. Its required for the forms and reports that you need to file taxes on their behalf. Get your teams names, dates of birth, addresses, employment start or end dates, tax filing numbers and compensation methods in writing. Then fill out and submit for verification Forms I-9, for employment eligibility, as well as W-4s or W-9s (for employees and independent contractors, respectively). Gathering these forms and information is critical to remaining compliant with state and federal laws surrounding small business payroll systems."},{"type":"h2","text":"3\\. Employee Classification"},{"type":"p","text":"Speaking of employees versus independent contractors, you’ll need to classify your employees to determine the legalities surrounding their wages, including what you owe and withhold in taxes. Are they full or part time? Are they a tipped worker?"},{"type":"p","text":"Tipped employees have their own associated laws which determine how they’re paid and what you owe the IRS. The U.S. Department of Labor defines tipped workers as anyone who makes more than $30 per month in tips, and they allow employers to pay tipped hourly workers less than the federal minimum wage ($7.25/hr) with a tip credit, meaning you only pay them $2.13/hr. The requirements for tip credits vary by state. Tips as taxable wages also depend on how much employees earn per month. It’s best to set up a tip-tracking system, like a daily report, to keep your books organized and make for easier filing come tax time."},{"type":"p","text":"Distinctions between employee classifications can be difficult, but they matter a lot. Incorrect filing can lead to large back pay sums, interests and penalties. Determining exempt versus nonexempt employees also informs their eligibility for things like benefits and overtime. The IRS provides Form SS-8 to help you with all of this so your accounting is a little bit less stressful."},{"type":"h2","text":"4\\. Pay Period"},{"type":"p","text":"Payday, the most important day of an employee’s fortnightor month. It’s not set in stone when you have to dole out payroll, so it’s up to you to decide. However there are a few things to consider before setting the date:"},{"type":"p","text":"1. State and federal restrictions."},{"type":"p","text":"2. Your own schedule. Payroll is a massive expense for a small business, so you should work out when works best and be the least detrimental to cash flow."},{"type":"p","text":"3. Employees’ needs. In the restaurant industry especially, pay is low and benefits are far and few between, so your staff probably has cash flow requirements of their own. When deciding the frequency of payroll, keep their needs in mind too."},{"type":"p","text":"Communication is key. Once you figure out when and how often to do your payroll, inform your staff so that everyone remains on the same page."},{"type":"h2","text":"5\\. Payroll System"},{"type":"p","text":"Now that you have the details sorted out, you can decide how you want to actually process your payroll. The choice really comes down to whether you want to do it manually or use a management software that takes care of everything for you."},{"type":"p","text":"Doing payroll by hand is definitely cheaper, but it also leaves plenty of room for human error and takes up a lot of time; managers waste multiple hours every month this way. If you invest in a software that handles payroll instead, you ultimately make back the upfront costs that you spend on a subscription. Companies like Gusto seamlessly integrate with your Point of Sale system when you sign up with eatOS. They automatically run payroll for you while giving you access to real time information, organizing your data, helping with taxes and making accounting management easy."},{"type":"p","text":"Whether you use Gusto or some other software, look at the reviews online and research what will work for your business. Your needs and budget determines what payroll management software fits best."},{"type":"p","text":"Though your payroll system is all set up and ready to use, your job still isnt over. You need to maintain best practices all the time so you never run into issues with the IRS. Some tips to remember:"},{"type":"p","text":"1. Pay on time, always. Late wages correlate to dips in staff morale."},{"type":"p","text":"2. Take your time and ensure payroll is completely correct. Over- or underpaying employees causes issues during tax season and will be a headache to unravel."},{"type":"p","text":"3. File taxes by the IRS deadline or risk a 10% Failure to Deposit penalty. Remember to file your city and state taxes, too."},{"type":"p","text":"4. File your Employer’s Quarterly Federal Tax Return form and report any withholdings. In line with pristine record-keeping, make copies of all of the forms mentioned in this article and store them securely for easy access."},{"type":"p","text":"Your payroll system is an integral part of regular business operations, and taking these steps to set it up properly will streamline the process down the line and ensure your restaurant is always square with finances, compliant with the law and ready to face the day."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_2dba80a003be4763a1555a287b40da57~mv2.jpeg', 'Workforce Management', 'eatOS Staff', '2022-03-31', 'published'),
  ('the-many-hats-of-restaurant-ownership', 'The Many Hats of Restaurant Ownership', 'Restaurant ownership is hectic around the clock. From planning to open up your first small business to actually handling the day-to-day demands of the venture, you know how time-consuming, fast-paced and volatile the...', '[{"type":"p","text":"Restaurant ownership is hectic around the clock. From planning to open up your first small business to actually handling the day-to-day demands of the venture, you know how time-consuming, fast-paced and volatile the restaurant industry is. You’ve got a lot on your plate, and you need to make constant, quick decisions about what deserves your attention the most."},{"type":"p","text":"At the top of the chain of command, there’s no need to sweat the small stuff. Part of your job is knowing where your attention belongs. Don’t take on jobs like inventory, clearing tables or running the bar: All of that should be delegated to junior employees."},{"type":"p","text":"Understanding the delicate ins and outs of your business means learning how to do every job in the restaurant (and what value each position contributes) even though you won’t actually perform these tasks on a day-to-day basis. Supervisors need to know what to look for so they can monitor employees’ progress and make informed decisions regarding workforce management, budgeting and more. Hire more competent candidates who you can trust to handle the restaurant in your absence, so you can focus on the priorities that require an owner’s touch."},{"type":"h2","text":"An Owner’s Duties"},{"type":"p","text":"If you’re not working the floor, back in the kitchens or training your managers, what should you be doing? Now you know how not to spend your time, and that’s because there are plenty of jobs that only the head of the chain of command can address. For example:"},{"type":"p","text":"1. Budgeting. You need to stay on top of your books to know how the business is doing and make appropriate changes depending on what you see. That means learning not just to read numbers, but analyze them too. Invest in superior technology like the Point of Sale powered by eatOS and get automatic reporting features that run sales data for you, so you can quickly and easily address areas of the business that need improvement as well as recognize what’s going well."},{"type":"p","text":"2. Establish goals and how to reach them. Set out expectations to give everyone guidelines to follow and reach their collective potential. This is especially useful for restaurateurs seeking to scale eventually, as they can see their own progress and determine if they’re prepared for the financial and mental strain of a second venue. eatOS technologies streamline operations, cut expenses and improve sales so you have a bigger safety net to experiment with a new concept, menu or marketing strategy."},{"type":"p","text":"3. Lead by example. Show your management team how to hold the reigns when you’re not around or otherwise occupied. If they approach your junior staff with the same open communication and mentor-like relationship as you show management, workplace morale will naturally grow as your team evolves more and more into a tight-knit unit all focused on the restaurant’s success."},{"type":"p","text":"The Many Hats of Restaurant Ownership"},{"type":"p","text":"When you delegate lesser tasks to junior employees where they belong, you free up your own time to focus on the restaurant’s branding, long-term direction, reputation and overall profitability. Taking time to plot out where you want your restaurant to grow gives you some clarity when making day to day decisions. Checkpoints can also be motivation in and of themselves: You’ll know you’re headed in the right direction when you’re making a certain amount of money each quarter, so when the day comes when you’re steadily profitable, celebrate with a special dinner promotion or by solidifying a plan to scale the business."},{"type":"p","text":"When you draw clear distinctions between each role, you strengthen the authority of the whole chain of command. This guarantees greater productivity and guides your ultimate success. Establishing each role’s duties can coexist with a mentor-like management style and reciprocal respect; these aren’t mutually exclusive concepts. Improve long-term profits with a clearer idea of your role as an owner and where everyone stands in the business."},{"type":"p","text":"The long-term direction of your restaurant relies on your clear understanding of what’s going on on the floor, how the business is doing overall and what trends are currently affecting the industry as a whole. Strengthen your role as an owner and watch your business naturally flourish as a result. Learn how advanced technology can help your restaurant rise to greatness with better communication, cutting-edge analytical software and more when you book a demo with eatOS."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant #RestaurantManagement"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_858081c372f24cb29b0f3653a54e4dfb~mv2.jpg', 'Workforce Management', 'eatOS Staff', '2022-03-30', 'published'),
  ('the-new-approach-to-culinary-training', 'The New Approach to Culinary Training', 'The restaurant industry underwent a lot of change during the pandemic. As they get ready to reopen for good, customer expectations and the overall dining experience remains permanently changed. Certain behaviors and...', '[{"type":"p","text":"The restaurant industry underwent a lot of change during the pandemic. As they get ready to reopen for good, customer expectations and the overall dining experience remains permanently changed. Certain behaviors and trends will stick around. Why not train up-and-coming workers differently from the start? Some culinary schools are thinking about that."},{"type":"p","text":"These students are experiencing an expanded curriculum that gives them the tools they’ll need to thrive in the new market. Soon, culinary schools may all train chefs with the new, new normal in mind."},{"type":"p","text":"Photo by MealPro on Unsplash"},{"type":"h2","text":"New Skills for New Workers"},{"type":"p","text":"Off-premise ordering is more popular than before. Contactless pickup and delivery options flourished during COVID-19, and now guests often prefer them. They’re both easy ways to enjoy their favorite meals. Thus, culinary training shouldn’t focus exclusively on satisfying dine-in customers anymore."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Learn proper presentation within the confines of a to-go box. When you have limited contact with the customer, restaurants can still show off their best side."},{"type":"p","text":"2. Start recipe training in foods that travel well. Delivery radiuses vary and you never know how far someone drove for a carry-out order. Menu items that will make it home will mean a world of difference when it comes to brand loyalty."},{"type":"p","text":"3. Create signature dishes that can be eaten on the go."},{"type":"p","text":"4. Now that face-to-face interactions are more limited, service workers have adopted unique ways to represent the company in a positive light."},{"type":"p","text":"Learning online is a new skill in and of itself, as many classes went virtual this year. As culinary training changes, why not use this as an opportunity to prepare a new kind of worker?"},{"type":"h2","text":"Culinary Training and Personal Skills"},{"type":"p","text":"Employees’ ability to work as a team determines your restaurant’s success. Cohesion means greater communication and staff retention, as well as customer satisfaction overall. Restaurants now know just how important this is when the going gets rough. Thus, students are learning ways to improve interpersonally as well as in the kitchen."},{"type":"p","text":"Discipline, professionalism, and flexibility all work together to make better employees. As customers show increasing interest in the environment and individual equity, restaurants will show their support more and more too. Expect vegetarian alternatives and more plant-based dishes to arise, which aspiring culinary stars should learn to recreate."},{"type":"p","text":"Soon, restaurants may start hiring chefs who graduated already well-versed in all the changes restaurants had to make to survive the pandemic. Make sure your restaurant is equipped with the best workforce management capabilities, so new hires can hit the ground running every shift."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #Covid19Pandemic #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_4f16d1df7d564ffc9f1a57393fe4bb8b~mv2.jpg', 'Online Ordering', 'eatOS Staff', '2022-03-25', 'published'),
  ('the-power-of-daily-lunch-specials', 'The Power of Daily Lunch Specials', 'Restaurants often overlook the importance of lunch specials because their key demographics might be busy during the day. If midday during the week isn’t one of your peak hours, you should consider building a following...', '[{"type":"p","text":"Restaurants often overlook the importance of lunch specials because their key demographics might be busy during the day. If midday during the week isn’t one of your peak hours, you should consider building a following that enjoys stopping by for lunch. This can supplement revenue made during busier hours and on the weekends."},{"type":"p","text":"Maximize lunch profits by paring down your target audience. Are you marketing to business professionals? College students between classes? How long do they have for lunch after their commute from the office? All of this informs what campaigns you’ll run and what avenues you’ll use for marketing these services."},{"type":"h2","text":"Creating Good Lunch Specials"},{"type":"p","text":"If the afternoon crowd tends to linger, you might consider building a special drinks menu for them to glance over while they wait. If they need to get back to business quickly, take time out of staff meetings to encourage employees to streamline their customer service, perhaps spending less time on chit-chat so they’ll improve turnaround."},{"type":"p","text":"Prix fixe lunch specials help. Busy crowds want fast service; reduce how long they spend browsing the menu by offering a few fixed price choices. Get meals to their tables faster as well by streamlining the rush and providing the efficient service they need. Happy customers will come back on their next lunch break."},{"type":"p","text":"Sometimes it can be difficult to garner new business. Give a taste of your food to the neighborhood with free samples alongside copies of the menu, so your target demographic so they’ll think of you next time they want to find a new place to eat. With a limited time for lunch, they might not want to waste their break somewhere that turns out unsatisfactory. Samples give them a chance to know whether that house special will hit the spot."},{"type":"p","text":"Delivery is a saving grace for customers whose commute prevents them from becoming a loyal regular. The walk to your restaurant might take up enough of their lunch hour that they would rather go somewhere just down the street. Offer lunchtime delivery, including group specials so coworkers can go in on a bulk order together for a discount. Include a takeout menu and any promotions you want to offer with each delivery so they’ll keep you in mind."},{"type":"p","text":"From start to finish, streamline the lunch process. Meals aren’t over when the plates are clean; the customer experience ends when they walk out the door, so even the transaction experience should be fast and contact-free. Smart technology like the Point of Sales powered by eatOS enables mobile pay for touchless transactions and even auto-generates tip percentages for added efficiency. Don’t make customers wait for the check; let people pay up front with online ordering capabilities."},{"type":"h2","text":"Advertising Your Way to Success"},{"type":"p","text":"Once you work out the best deals to generate revenue, you need to promote these new endeavors. Your servers are your salesforce and should push the lunch specials during breakfast and dinner to make sure every customer knows you’re ready for midday delivery."},{"type":"p","text":"In addition to in-person upsales, take advantage of mobile ads that cater to the modern consumer. Whether they catch someone’s eye during a long commute or through mutual social media followers, online channels matter. People are spending more time than usual browsing the internet during quarantine. Use geofencing to advertise to customers in an appropriate radius and watch your lunch specials begin to pull in much-needed extra profit."},{"type":"p","text":"Smart restaurants rely on advanced technology to streamline operations and improve their efficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #RestaurantBusiness"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_7b40f8dc8c034ea0a3fda8f06be41573~mv2.jpg', 'Workforce Management', 'eatOS Staff', '2022-03-23', 'published'),
  ('the-restaurant-technology-starter-guide', 'The Restaurant Technology Starter Guide', 'When your restaurant systems have been set in stone for awhile, change can seem like too much work. New restaurant technology may cost a lot and take time to train on. But upgrading your devices leaves you better off in...', '[{"type":"p","text":"When your restaurant systems have been set in stone for awhile, change can seem like too much work. New restaurant technology may cost a lot and take time to train on. But upgrading your devices leaves you better off in the long run."},{"type":"p","text":"Once you learn how to use the tech and streamline onboarding, you’ll reduce the stress of employee turnover when it occurs. Staff retention also improves with better communication and workforce management features. Overall, top-of-the-line restaurant technology available today creates happier customers and more efficient businesses."},{"type":"p","text":"Photo courtesy of eatOS"},{"type":"h2","text":"Creating Strong Goals"},{"type":"p","text":"Your mission informs what tech you need to reach your full potential. Do you want to have the lowest prices in the area? Or pull in more Gen Z customers than anyone else? Or maybe grow to eventually open another location? Whatever your dream for the restaurant, really conceptualize it to know what to invest in."},{"type":"p","text":"The best tech will always be the kind that adapts with your business as it grows. The market doesn’t stay the same, so you can’t expect your restaurant to, either. With eatOS, we run automatic reports that analyze success over time. When you implement changes, we analyze the effect on profit for you. Keep what works and overhaul what doesn’t faster, for a healthier business overall."},{"type":"h2","text":"Define What Customers Want"},{"type":"p","text":"Consider what consumers want and start there. Right now, delivery and takeout is extremely important because it’s a safe way to get delicious food. If you’re not sure where to start with new tech, maybe add an app to go with your website landing page for online orders. This will appeal to all those customers who prefer using their phone for convenience. eatOS Point of Sale collects orders from all your channels and links them to the Kitchen Display Screen together, and queue ordering makes sure no customer waits too long."},{"type":"p","text":"All of this also allows your staff to communicate more seamlessly with each other, improving both their efficiency and overall job satisfaction. Technology doesn’t just have positive ROI; it makes your customers, and your workforce, more likely to stay."},{"type":"h2","text":"Restaurant Technology: A Worthwhile Investment?"},{"type":"p","text":"Although the upfront costs can seem mountainous, it’s the return on investment that really matters. Whatever you spend on a Self-Service Kiosk, for example, you’ll make back after one shift of what you’d normally pay a worker. That’s another reason training your staff in how to use the equipment is so important, so the transition is seamless."},{"type":"p","text":"When buying new restaurant technology, prioritize what can scale with your business and most effectively help the brand grow. It’s not always about what’s hot on the market, but what’s best for the restaurant going forward."},{"type":"p","text":"#RestaurantTechnologyandCOVID19 #eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_a07f456a336f4f37aeb676598dbe6d59~mv2.jpg', 'Workforce Management', 'eatOS Staff', '2022-03-22', 'published'),
  ('the-secret-to-cutting-restaurant-costs', 'The Secret to Cutting Restaurant Costs', 'Running a restaurant is a costly operation. From always ordering ingredients in bulk to compensating staff members appropriately, there are plenty of unavoidable expenses that come from running a business; you’re...', '[{"type":"p","text":"Running a restaurant is a costly operation. From always ordering ingredients in bulk to compensating staff members appropriately, there are plenty of unavoidable expenses that come from running a business; you’re probably looking to cut costs wherever you possibly can. More often, restaurants are recognizing the potential in Self-Ordering Kiosks to reduce costs and make more profit at the same time."},{"type":"p","text":"How is that possible? The self-service trend rose over the past several years and then exploded into what it is today. What began as a hit among commercialized chain restaurants has grown in popularity to the point where customers consider it almost an expectation more than a perk; at the same time, technology has advanced to a place where Kiosks are much more common than they were at the outset. At eatOS, we’re committed to designing affordable devices with features to handle everything you need to do. From better staff management to a more productive workforce, eatOS Kiosks are designed with your business in mind."},{"type":"p","text":"As Kiosks become household names, the decline in exclusivity and commercialized spread should let the financial benefits of incorporating a Self-Order Kiosk into your restaurant operations to speak for themselves."},{"type":"h2","text":"Streamline Operations"},{"type":"p","text":"Cost benefits aside, restaurants run smoother when you integrate self-service capabilities. Putting customers in control of their tickets gives them ample time to peruse the menu and a chance to check over their order before they put down a credit card, which ensures more accurate orders. Additionally, eatOS Kiosks integrate into your network and update your sales data in real-time, so orders go directly to your Point of Sale and Kitchen Display Systems."},{"type":"p","text":"KDS skip the middleman and send orders straight to a screen set up in the kitchen, where cooks can see everything in the queue, how long orders have been there, and prioritize their tasks accordingly, so every dish goes out promptly. This increases table turn times and naturally raises your profits as a result."},{"type":"p","text":"From the customer side of the equation, they appreciate self-service options for many reasons: Getting to cut around lines reduces their wait for food, which aligns well with younger generations’ on-the-go attitude. However, everyone appreciates shorter wait times, greater accuracy, and total control over their dining experience. Kiosks beget happier customers, and that increases the likelihood that they’ll become a loyal patron for life."},{"type":"h2","text":"Save Money, Make Money"},{"type":"p","text":"You’ve heard the old saying, “you need to spend money to make money”? With Kiosks, you can save money _and make_ more at the same time. Thanks in large part to the time guests spend with the menu and forced modifiers and automatic upselling features; Kiosks raise the average ticket size by approximately 10%."},{"type":"p","text":"They might also be the push someone needs to submit an order in the first place. When you’re very busy, a long line might make new walk-ins turn around and go someplace else. Guest profiles make it easy for returning customers to reorder their usuals quickly as well; Kiosks shorten lines, so you don’t miss out on that customer who’s pressed for time."},{"type":"p","text":"Despite all these perks, perhaps you’re worried about the downpayment for an initial investment; it’s only fair to be cautious. This is why demos are beneficial, so you can be sure you’re choosing the right vendor whose product suits your specific needs. However, there is some truth to that popular phrase about spending money: Kiosks will save you many labor costs. The minimum wage has been steadily rising all around the country, and if you can replace even one worker per day, then you’ll make back your investment with eatOS in one or two shifts, not that you’ll need to worry about scheduling. eatOS devices are integrated with 7Shifts, superior workforce management software that makes creating and changing schedules fast and easy."},{"type":"p","text":"If you’re thinking about a Self-Ordering Kiosk, don’t hesitate: The return on investment is more than worth it."},{"type":"h2","text":"Consumer Preferences"},{"type":"p","text":"The needs and expectations of the typical restaurant patron have evolved a lot in the past year. This pandemic shaped the industry landscape in a push toward contactless service options and creative business models that prioritize health and safety just as much as great food."},{"type":"p","text":"Younger generations are spilling en masse into the marketplace now, and their priorities matter. Millennials put trillions of dollars into the industry already, and Gen Z is beginning to graduate college and spend their disposable income on good takeout. Don’t write any of them off; hospitality as a whole is changing trajectories, and self-service is now just as big a part of so many customers’ preferred dining experiences as good staff, and quality service still is. Consumer values now include speed and safety just as much as delicious food and smiling waiters."},{"type":"p","text":"Some restaurants saw how much customers enjoy self-sufficiency and decided to take it to the next level: Restaurateurs have begun experimenting with Kiosk-only restaurants that rely entirely on modern technology. However, QSRs, fast-casual concepts, and cafés don’t have a monopoly on self-ordering: Even fine dining establishments see the upsides of incorporating Kiosks into everyday operations."},{"type":"p","text":"Don’t underestimate the power of self-service in a modern restaurant. Running a business in today’s world is a balancing act: Speed with convenience, safety with efficiency, taste with healthy living."},{"type":"p","text":"Self-Service Kiosks are the answer. Schedule a demo with eatOS, and we’ll show you how our workforce management capabilities, durability, efficiency, and third-party integrations make your operations run better than ever before. 2021 will be the year of self-service. Is your restaurant ready to meet a new day?"},{"type":"p","text":"#SelfServiceKiosks #eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant #WorkforceManagement"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_cd0a6a84ca5048f38b0d02562ff3c558~mv2.jpg', 'Self-Service Kiosk', 'eatOS Staff', '2022-03-17', 'published'),
  ('tips-for-hiring-seasonal-restaurant-workers', 'Tips for Hiring Seasonal Restaurant Workers', 'Skip to Main Content', '[{"type":"p","text":"Skip to Main Content"},{"type":"p","text":"Few things are certain in the restaurant industry, but one thing’s for sure: It gets busy during the holiday season. This upswing in customer traffic means you’ll need extra hands around the restaurant to accommodate demand, which means hiring seasonal workers to keep operations running as smoothly as possible."},{"type":"p","text":"Knowing that the winter holidays are coming is actually beneficial to you as an owner because it means you can get a jumpstart on the hiring process. Take ample time to train your temporary hires and make sure they fully understand how to work in your restaurant. You also get the best talent when you recruit earlier than your competition, making sure you have the strongest workforce available when the winter comes."},{"type":"h2","text":"Why do you need to hire seasonal workers?"},{"type":"p","text":"Restaurants usually reach out for added staff during the summer or winter months. Your busy seasons naturally depend on where you’re located; restaurants near attractions like beaches, amusement parks, and big cities tend to get more families visiting when the children are off school for the summer. Meanwhile, winter traffic usually picks up for restaurants in places that are still sunny in December or alternatively in cold ski towns. Advanced Point of Sale technology powered by eatOS identifies your busiest times of the year by analyzing past traffic so you have a better understanding of what to expect this coming season."},{"type":"p","text":"Before you start recruiting, identify the roles that you’ll need filling over the break. Typically, owners hire more servers, cooks or delivery workers but every business is different; you know best what positions get overwhelmed when it’s busy. Hire specifically to keep up with the added demand but still keep labor costs low the rest of the year."},{"type":"p","text":"You’ll save money on more than just the usual expenses: Seasonal workers tend to be students with less experience who are seeking part-time work; restaurants typically pay them less than full-time, permanent workers. All of this keeps labor costs manageable because youll also reduce spending during the rest of the year. Work smarter, not harder."},{"type":"h2","text":"How can you help seasonal workers succeed?"},{"type":"p","text":"Identify what roles your restaurant will lack after accommodating your regular staff taking some well-deserved time off for Thanksgiving, the winter holidays, and New Years’. Ask your year-round workers about their plans early so you have plenty of time to make a new schedule and get a clear idea of what positions will need to be filled."},{"type":"p","text":"The sooner you put out job postings, the better candidates you’ll get answering the call. Don’t settle: Create listings two to three months in advance of when you actually need coverage. Post on nearby universities’ job boards, get together with other restaurants in the area to participate in hiring fairs, search out industry-specific job sites and ask your current workers for referrals. Offering referral bonuses is a great incentive and an easy way to find qualified temporary employees."},{"type":"p","text":"Hiring seasonal workers isn’t like hiring for permanent positions. Prioritize flexibility and availability on busy holidays and in the evening. Make sure students’ university schedules line up with your needs since there are no uniform regulations regarding finals. Oftentimes, temporary restaurant positions get filled by international students so remember to take time in advance to sponsor them as well."},{"type":"p","text":"While it can be tempting to give away roles to the first qualified candidates that you find, remember that there’s a lot more that goes into each position than pure experience. For example, if you have two servers with an equal background in the industry, youd want to prioritize the one whos previously worked with the kinds of people you get at your own restaurant. You need workers who understand how to cater to your demographics, whether that’s families who like to spend big or college students with empty pockets."},{"type":"p","text":"Once you’ve found your temporary hires, it’s time to train them as quickly and efficiently as possible for the upcoming winter rush. A mass orientation is the most effective way to get everybody up to speed at the same time. Its good to train them on your operations during normal periods so they can get experience working your floor before graduating to a rush. It’s a good idea to have new trainees do a couple regular shifts with permanent workers, if you can, so they learn the ropes during downtimes next to somebody experienced at the job."},{"type":"p","text":"Regardless of preparedness, keep managers close by for support and resources during the holiday season to handle questions from guests or staff and to smooth over any bumpy transitions."},{"type":"p","text":"Seasonal workers are necessary to successfully get through the busy season when your usual employees are submitting time-off requests but eager vacationers are pouring in by the minivan. Hire your seasonal workers now so you’ve got time to train them and make sure they start off on the right note this holiday season."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #Holidays #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurants #StaffingampRecruiting"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_07886140fd5d4142a19af4c8ac888bf7~mv2.jpeg', 'Workforce Management', 'eatOS Staff', '2022-03-16', 'published');
